     "created_at": "2025-05-08T21:58:25Z",
     "updated_at": "2025-05-08T21:58:25Z",
     "body": "Beginning to be concerned about security implications... need to do more design work and present this to the TAG.\n\nConcerns include:\n\n* Ensuring all queries filter by tenant. _This might not be much of a problem since we have only a small number of queries, without any possibility of ad hoc queries_.\n* Shared web server and caching.\n\n---\n\nSchema-based segregation would be safer than column-based. That works for PostgreSQL, but not OpenSearch / Elasticsearch.\n\nSchema-based segregation would also lead to an explosion of Kafka connector configurations each monitoring different tables.\n\n---\n\nSome references and thoughts on potential OS / ES multi-tenancy; number of shards per index is a big consideration.\n\n* https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp-sharding.html\n* https://www.elastic.co/blog/found-sizing-elasticsearch\n* https://www.elastic.co/blog/found-multi-tenancy\n* https://bigdataboutique.com/blog/multi-tenancy-with-elasticsearch-and-opensearch-c1047b\n\nSeparating tenants by index (kind of like schema segregation in RDBMS) is problematic because you end up with lots of small shards, which each take up some baseline of memory. Quickly run out of memory. This might work with a small number of tenants but probably needs robust testing even then.\n\nLarge tenants should probably be on their own, not mixed with other tenants, to avoid that one hogging the resources. One article suggests using custom routing with a tenant ID as routing key.\n\nCreating a [filtered index](https://docs.opensearch.org/docs/latest/im-plugin/index-alias/#create-filtered-aliases) per tenant could be a little tedious up-front, but would pay off in terms of _looking_ like schema segregation without actually being schema segregation.",
     "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
